If you want to truly understand how Spark works internally, you need to understand DAG.

This is one of the most important concepts for interviews and performance tuning.

⸻

What is DAG?

A DAG (Directed Acyclic Graph) is a logical execution plan created by Spark.

It represents how your data transformations are connected and executed.
	•	Directed → Flow has a direction (start → end)
	•	Acyclic → No loops (no infinite cycles)
	•	Graph → Nodes (operations) + Edges (data flow)

⸻

How Spark Uses DAG

When you write PySpark code:

df = spark.read.csv("data.csv")

df1 = df.filter(df.age > 25)

df2 = df1.groupBy("city").count()

df2.show()

What happens internally?

1️⃣ Spark records transformations
2️⃣ Builds a DAG (logical plan)
3️⃣ Optimizes using Catalyst Optimizer
4️⃣ Splits into stages
5️⃣ Executes tasks across executors

Read Data
   ↓
Filter (Transformation)
   ↓
GroupBy (Transformation)
   ↓
Action (Trigger)
   ↓
Execution Starts

DAG Components

🔹 Nodes

Each operation (filter, map, groupBy)

🔹 Edges

Flow of data between operations

🔹 Stages

Grouped tasks separated by shuffle boundaries

⸻

Why DAG is Important

✅ 1. Optimized Execution

Spark analyzes the full workflow before execution

✅ 2. Better Performance

Reduces unnecessary computations

✅ 3. Fault Tolerance

If a task fails, Spark recomputes only that part using DAG

⸻
Interview Point :

👉 Spark does not execute step-by-step like traditional systems

Instead, it:

✔ Builds DAG
✔ Optimizes it
✔ Executes efficiently in parallel
